Project contenthub {
  Note: '''
    ContentHub 1.0: connection-authorized retrieval with acknowledged-delivery
    deduplication. Standalone replacement design, not an executable migration.
    Earlier checkpoints and any existing archive data are not deleted.

    Two tables: subscriptions and delivery_attempts.
    No persisted content, raw payloads, cleaned results, item registry,
    memberships, content corrections, durable queues, cursors, or run recovery.
    Agents/connections/grants are proposed owner-file configuration with stable
    IDs; agents cannot change grants or credentials through subscription tools.
    No database FK points to owner configuration. Validate live references.
    Historical subscription_id on attempts is intentionally not a FK:
    deleting a subscription keeps its ledger records. Never reuse IDs.

    Single-selector subscriptions are editable at runtime by their owner.
    A selector may accept several targets of the same supported type.
    Initial collection is proposed pull-driven, not automatic startup polling.
    Shared bounded asyncio discovery/fetch queues serve all agents without
    cross-agent content sharing. Preparation runs inside the fetch task.
    Runtime buffers and work disappear on restart; the ledger is not a queue.

    All direct/subscription fetches require explicit acknowledgment.
    Only retained acknowledgments suppress new requests for that agent/item.
    Raw/cleaned representation is recorded but excluded from item identity.
    Direct repeats return already_served unless allow_repeat explicitly
    requests a fresh fetch. A repeat creates another attempt and acknowledgment.
    Fetch/processing errors are diagnostic, not persistent retry blockers.
    Current authorization, target verification, and resource checks still apply.

    UTC timestamps, application-generated UUID strings, and SQLite metadata
    storage are proposed initially. DBML notes require implementation checks;
    compiling this schema does not validate authorization or runtime semantics.
  '''
}

Table subscriptions {
  id varchar(36) [pk, not null]
  agent_id varchar(36) [not null, note: 'Immutable owning agent ID from owner configuration; not caller-selected authority']
  name varchar(255) [not null]
  connection_id varchar(36) [not null, note: 'One currently granted stable connection ID; live validity checked by service']
  selector_type varchar(128) [not null, note: 'Exactly one supported type, e.g. youtube_channels, youtube_playlists, gmail_query']
  selector_json json [not null, note: 'Validated arguments for one selector type; may contain multiple same-type targets']
  content_kind varchar(64) [not null]
  options_json json [not null, note: 'Normalized material requested-content options; {} when none; excludes representation']
  representation varchar(16) [not null, note: 'raw | cleaned']
  lookback_seconds bigint [not null, note: 'Positive default collection window duration, shared by all selector targets']
  created_at timestamp [not null]
  updated_at timestamp [not null]

  indexes {
    (agent_id, name) [unique]
    (agent_id, connection_id)
  }

  Note: '''
    Agent-owned active collection rule, not archive permission or YAML registry.
    One connection, selector type, content request, representation, and lookback.
    Multiple channels or multiple playlists are valid when supported;
    channels plus playlists are separate subscriptions.
    Validate owner grants, selector capabilities, options and positive lookback
    on creation/update. Snapshots of admitted requests are runtime-only.
    Updates affect later requests; no settings-revision history is required.
    Owner identity and subscription ID are immutable; timestamps track rule edits.
    Missing/revoked configured references make this rule unusable, not deleted.
    Deletion leaves delivery_attempts and acknowledgments intact and does not
    implicitly cancel already admitted work. Names are unique per owner only.
    No schedule column or automatic polling is required in the initial proposal.
    Selector JSON is not a payload cache, secret store, or provider scan cursor.
  '''
}

Table delivery_attempts {
  id varchar(36) [pk, not null, note: 'Opaque attempt/delivery ID used for acknowledgment; new for every actual repeat']
  agent_id varchar(36) [not null, note: 'Requesting agent; all ledger access is owner-scoped']
  connection_id varchar(36) [not null, note: 'Actual authorized execution route, diagnostic rather than item identity']
  subscription_id varchar(36) [note: 'Optional originating subscription ID; historical metadata without a live FK']

  source varchar(64) [not null]
  upstream_kind varchar(64) [not null]
  namespace varchar(255) [not null, note: 'Verified stable upstream identity boundary; explicit empty string for supported public content']
  source_id varchar(512) [not null]
  content_kind varchar(64) [not null]
  options_json json [not null, note: 'Canonical material content options, {} when none; raw/cleaned is not an option here']
  item_key varchar(64) [not null, note: '64 lowercase hex SHA-256 of canonical six-field identity; deliberately NOT unique']

  representation varchar(16) [not null, note: 'raw | cleaned; either acknowledged representation suppresses the same item']
  attempted_at timestamp [not null, note: 'Actual item-attempt start; row is persisted on terminal fetch/preparation outcome']
  fetching_status varchar(16) [not null, note: 'fetched | error; diagnostics only, not queued/running or retry eligibility']
  fetch_error_code varchar(128)
  fetch_error_message text [note: 'Sanitized diagnostic; no payloads, credentials or raw provider dumps']
  processing_error_code varchar(128) [note: 'Fetch succeeded but requested output preparation/validation failed']
  processing_error_message text [note: 'Sanitized preparation diagnostic only']
  delivery_time timestamp [note: 'Complete-item response handoff attempt; NULL before complete delivery or on failure']
  acknowledgement_time timestamp [note: 'First explicit acknowledgment of this delivery; only this field enables suppression']

  indexes {
    (agent_id, item_key, acknowledgement_time)
    (agent_id, attempted_at, id)
    (agent_id, subscription_id, attempted_at)
    (agent_id, connection_id, attempted_at)
    acknowledgement_time
    attempted_at
  }

  Note: '''
    One metadata row per terminal item retrieval attempt, not per canonical item.
    No uniqueness on agent_id/item_key: retries and forced repeats coexist.
    A crash before terminal insertion can leave no attempt record.
    No rows for discovery-only references, acknowledged-item skips, or
    pre-admission validation rejection. This is not a recoverable work log.

    Identity is source + upstream_kind + namespace + source_id + content_kind
    + normalized material options_json, hashed as structured canonical UTF-8 JSON.
    Exclude agent, subscription, provider/connection route, representation,
    delivery timing, retry/override settings, and parser implementation version.
    Compare full identity on a hash match; mismatch is an integrity error.
    Normalize defaults and ordering centrally; canonicalization must be stable.

    Check any retained same-agent matching-identity acknowledged row for
    suppression, regardless of route, subscription, or raw/cleaned representation.
    Permission and upstream target verification precede the check.
    An explicit override fetches again and creates another row; failed repeats
    never overwrite or erase older acknowledgment evidence.

    Fetching error requires fetch_error_code and no processing error,
    delivery_time, or acknowledgement_time. Fetched requires no fetch errors.
    Processing errors require fetched and imply no delivery/acknowledgment.
    Error messages are nullable; a message requires its corresponding code.
    Successful fetched/prepared rows may remain undelivered/unacknowledged.
    No field here implements an exhausted retry allowance or permanent blocker.

    Attempt identity/context, outcomes, and attempted_at are immutable after
    insertion. delivery_time and acknowledgement_time each transition only
    from NULL to their first assigned UTC timestamp; repeated ack is idempotent.
    Timestamp order: attempted_at <= delivery_time <= acknowledgement_time
    whenever these values exist. Enforce in transaction/check constraints.
    Acknowledgment requires fetched, no preparation error, complete delivery,
    matching agent ownership, and current authorization.

    Persist delivery_time immediately before final response/chunk handoff.
    This is an attempted handoff, not atomic proof of network delivery.
    Without acknowledgment, a lost response never suppresses a new request.
    For content continuation, only the final complete-item handoff qualifies.
    Acknowledge by delivery ID; memory/batch cursor state is not required after
    restart if the completed delivery row is retained and still authorized.
    Purged IDs are explicitly unknown/expired; never recreate lost receipts.

    No raw bytes, cleaned text, search text, content JSON, credentials, response
    envelopes, continuation tokens, queued statuses, or batch payloads here.
    Item metadata/error text must not become a hidden content archive.
    Acknowledged buffers are released; unacknowledged buffers expire in memory.
    Purge acknowledged rows by acknowledgement_time, not source publication date.
    Failed/unacknowledged rows may expire by attempted_at under a separate policy.
    Purging receipt evidence intentionally permits redelivery.
    Protect live deliveries from purge and serialize purge with acknowledgment.
    Historical subscription references are not permission grants and survive
    subscription deletion without an FK or retained subscription tombstone.
  '''
}
